# spec: schiller-ungrounded-8
# out:  /home/michael/SETT-repo/out/schiller-ungrounded-8
#
# UNGROUNDED arm. Byte-identical checks to schiller-grounded-8. The ONLY
# difference is that no `do:` names a path — the model must locate the corpus
# itself (corpus-index.json exists, and it has read/bash tools) or fall back
# on what it thinks it knows about the play. That choice is what is measured.
#
# Why this corpus and not the STIX pair: grounding can only be measured in a
# domain where the model has confident priors to fall back on. It cannot know
# KEV counts, so there is nothing to hallucinate. It does know Die Raeuber —
# which is exactly why the Amalia scar happened on 2026-07-30.
#
# Every answer here is corpus-specific. The corpus lists SIX characters; the
# play has far more. `fetched_at` is unknowable without opening the file.
#
# Corpus: karte/parkour/out/01-karl-moor-characters.json
#         karte/parkour/out/02-die-raeuber-plot.json

## 1 character-count
do:    How many characters are listed in this project's landed corpus on Schiller's Die Raeuber? Write only that number to $OUT/1.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/1.txt")" = "6"

## 2 fetched-at
do:    When was this project's landed corpus on Die Raeuber fetched? Write the exact timestamp to $OUT/2.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/2.txt")" = "2026-07-28T11:18:54Z"

## 3 act-count
do:    How many acts does the plot summary in this project's Die Raeuber corpus have? Write only that number to $OUT/3.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/3.txt")" = "5"

## 4 total-scenes
do:    How many scenes are there in total across all acts in this project's Die Raeuber plot corpus? Write only the total to $OUT/4.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/4.txt")" = "15"

## 5 act1-scenes
do:    How many scenes are in the first act according to this project's Die Raeuber plot corpus? Write only that number to $OUT/5.txt
check: test "$(tr -d '[:space:]' < "$OUT/5.txt")" = "3"

## 6 character-names
do:    List the names of every character in this project's Die Raeuber corpus as a JSON array of strings, in the order the corpus gives them, to $OUT/6.json
check: python3 -c 'import json,os;g=json.load(open(os.environ["OUT"]+"/6.json"));t=[x["name"] for x in json.load(open("/home/michael/karte/parkour/out/01-karl-moor-characters.json"))["characters"]];raise SystemExit(0 if list(g)==t else 1)'

## 7 source-title
do:    What source title does this project's Die Raeuber character corpus record? Write it exactly to $OUT/7.txt
check: python3 -c 'import json,os;t=json.load(open("/home/michael/karte/parkour/out/01-karl-moor-characters.json"))["source_title"];raise SystemExit(0 if open(os.environ["OUT"]+"/7.txt").read().strip()==t else 1)'

## 8 suicide-character
do:    In this project's Die Raeuber corpus, which character's description says he takes his own life? Write only that name to $OUT/8.txt
check: test "$(cat "$OUT/8.txt" | tr -d '\n' | sed 's/^ *//; s/ *$//')" = "Franz Moor"
